\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{A geometric countertest to topology alone.} For two particles remove collisions and keep the relative direction $n\in S^2$. For either $\varepsilon=1$ or $-1$, define
\[
 \mathcal H_\varepsilon=\{f\in L^2(S^2):f(-n)=\varepsilon f(n)\}.
\]
These are sections of the two flat complex line bundles over $\mathbb{RP}^2$ associated with the characters $\pm1$ of the deck transformation. Rotations act by $(U(R)f)(n)=f(R^{-1}n)$ and preserve each subspace because $R(-n)=-Rn$. This is a genuine representation of $SO(3)$, so a $2\pi$ rotation acts as the identity in both cases. Exchange instead acts by the independent sign $\varepsilon$.

\paragraph{A nonzero wrong-statistics sector.} The odd space is nonzero: $f(n)=n_3$ lies in it. It therefore describes spinless orbital wavefunctions with antisymmetric exchange, while obeying rotation covariance and the collision-free configuration-space construction. Adding a radial compactly supported factor and a center-of-mass wavefunction gives square-integrable two-particle states in the original three-dimensional setting. The free Laplacian preserves the exchange condition, since permutation and rotations commute with its differential expression.

\paragraph{What has and has not been disproved.} This does not contradict the physical spin--statistics theorem: no relativistic locality, positive-energy field algebra, or source-specific extra physical condition was imposed. It proves that the configuration bundle and ordinary rotation covariance alone cannot force Bose statistics for spin zero. A geometric proof needs an additional hypothesis identifying the exchange holonomy with the relevant spin rotation. Establishing a physically justified such hypothesis, then proving sufficiency and testing minimality, remains the exact missing step in both bundled source questions.

